## Claim
Text: "“Red-tagging” is a term invented by the Communist Party of the Philippines, the New People's Army, and the National Democratic Front (CPP-NPA-NDF)."
Claim author: Lorraine Badoy
Claim date: October 18, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### What is the established definition of "red-tagging" as used in Philippine political and human rights discourse?
Answer: According to the source, "Red-tagging in the Philippines" is established as "the malicious harassment and blacklisting of individuals or organizations critical or not fully supportive of the actions of a sitting government administration," where they are "tagged' as either communist or terrorist or both, regardless of their actual political beliefs or affiliations." Formal definitions include the Philippine Commission on Human Rights' adoption of a definition from the International Peace Observers Network (IPON) as "An act of State actors, particularly law enforcement agencies, to publicly brand individuals, groups, or institutions as… affiliated to communist or leftist terrorists," and a definition from Philippine jurisprudence as "the act of labelling, branding, naming and accusing individuals and/ or organizations of being left-leaning, subversives, communists or terrorists... by State agents, particularly law enforcement agencies and the military, against those perceived to be 'threats' or 'enemies of the State.'"

Source URL: https://www.wikiwand.com/en/Red-tagging_in_the_Philippines

