### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check refutes the claim because it finds no credible evidence that the Communist Party of the Philippines (CPP) or its affiliated groups coined the term "red-tagging." Instead, the term is a well-established concept in Philippine human rights discourse, defined by entities such as the [Philippine Commission on Human Rights](https://www.philstar.com/headlines/2019/10/07/1958232/how-activists-respond-being-tagged-rebels) and [Philippine jurisprudence](https://www.philstar.com/headlines/2020/03/09/1999465/eight-un-experts-raise-concerns-red-tagging-rights-workers) as a tactic used by state agents. Its usage is widespread among credible independent actors, including [UN human rights experts](https://www.philstar.com/headlines/2020/03/09/1999465/eight-un-experts-raise-concerns-red-tagging-rights-workers) and international media like the [BBC](https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-54144623), to describe a practice historically associated with Philippine governments and security forces, not with the CPP-NPA-NDF.